{
  "$comment": "A Burj-class reference building — GitHub issue #376, DECISIONS.md § D527, docs/38 § 2.5. 165 levels, 57 lifts, 8 escalators, speeds to 10 m/s. THE ARITHMETIC, so nobody re-derives it: two basements (B2, B1) plus G and floors 1–162 is 165 levels, of which 163 are at or above ground. Floors 154–162 are mechanical and carry no population; 2–153 are populated, which is 152 floors, and the reference's 'about 154 populated floors' is honoured as a shape rather than transcribed floor by floor — the six sky-lobby levels (43/44, 76/77, 123/124) are transfer floors and carry no residents either. POPULATION AND MIX ARE AN ASSUMPTION, NOT A CITATION, which § D527 leaves to this file: the per-floor figures fall from 29 in the lower office zone to 12 in the upper residential one, summing to 3198, and no published occupancy for the reference tower was available to the author. THE FIGURE IS MEASURED RATHER THAN GUESSED: it is what this 57-lift arrangement serves at `office-standard` with a valid AWT. Measured over a 1 800 s window, seed 376, `collective`: 3 198 people give a 35 s mean wait, a 98 s 95th percentile and nothing undelivered; 5 307 saturate (175 s, AWT suppressed) and 10 614 leave 422 of 4 457 journeys unserved at a 791 s mean. So a published occupancy higher than this does not merely change a field — it says the reference tower's real lift count is not 57 on this arrangement, which is the kind of thing this building exists to expose. Declared as an assumption so a later citation replaces a stated one rather than a silent one. The traffic profile is office-standard because the lower half is offices and the profile library ships nothing mixed-use with a supertall's shape; that too is an assumption. The reference is a floor, not a ceiling (§ D527).",
